For every real $s\ge0$, all three of the following hold:

1. the family $k\mapsto 1/(k+1)^s$, $k\in\mathbb N$ (real power), is summable if and only if $s>1$;
2. if $s>1$, then the complex numbers $\mathrm{genHarmonic}(N,s)=\sum_{k=1}^N 1/k^s$ (real, cast to $\mathbb C$) converge as $N\to\infty$ to $\zeta(s)$, Mathlib's Riemann zeta function evaluated at the complex number $s$;
3. if $s\le1$, then $\mathrm{genHarmonic}(N,s)\to+\infty$ as $N\to\infty$ (Lean `Tendsto … atTop atTop`).
